{"name":"Love Potion","hex":"#ce145e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"love potion","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A drink supposed to have the magic power to make the drinker fall in love with a specified person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/love_potion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Love potion","blocks":[{"text":"A love potion is a magical liquid which supposedly causes the drinker to develop feelings of love towards the person who served it. Another common term to describe the potion, philtre, is thought to have originated from the ancient Greek term philtron, via the French word philtre."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Evelyn_De_Morgan_-_The_Love_Potion_%281903%29_-_Google_Cultural_Institute.jpg/330px-Evelyn_De_Morgan_-_The_Love_Potion_%281903%29_-_Google_Cultural_Institute.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love_potion","wikidata":"Q2088342"},{"title":"Love Potion (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Love Potion\" is the twenty-first single by Japanese recording artist Alisa Mizuki. It was released on August 21, 2002, as the fifth single from Mizuki's fourth compilation album History: Alisa Mizuki Complete Single Collection. The title track is a Japanese-language cover Christina Aguilera's \"Genie in a Bottle\" (1998). The Japanese lyrics were written by Takeshi Aida (相田毅). The song served as theme song for the fourth and final season of the Fuji TV drama Nurse no Oshigoto."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9b/Alisa_Mizuki_-_Love_Potion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love_Potion_(song)","wikidata":"Q6691166"},{"title":"Love","blocks":[{"text":"Love encompasses a range of strong and positive emotional and mental states, from the most sublime virtue or good habit, the deepest interpersonal affection, to the simplest pleasure. An example of this range of meanings is that the love of a mother differs from the love of a spouse, which differs from the love for food. Most commonly, love refers to a feeling of strong attraction and emotional attachment."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love","wikidata":"Q316"},{"title":"Potion","blocks":[{"text":"A potion is a liquid that contains \"medicine, poison, or something that is supposed to have magic powers.\" It derives from the Latin word potio which refers to a drink or the act of drinking. The term philtre is also used, often specifically to describe a love potion, a potion that is believed to induce feelings of love or attraction in the one who drinks it.\nThroughout history, there have been several types of potions for a range of purposes. Reasons for taking potions have included curing an illness, securing immortality, and trying to inspire love. These potions, while often ineffective or poisonous, occasionally had some degree of medicinal benefits depending on what they sought to fix and the type and amount of ingredients used. Common ingredients in historical potions included Spanish fly, nightshade plants, cannabis, and opium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Filtre_d%27Amour.jpg/330px-Filtre_d%27Amour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potion","wikidata":"Q1249918"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/500px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/1280px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg/500px-Columpio_Veracruz_059.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fraternal love (Prehispanic sculpture from 250 to 900 CE, of Huastec origin). Museum of Anthropology in Xalapa, Veracruz, Mexico","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColumpio_Veracruz_059.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg/500px-Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg/500px-Gothaer_Liebespaar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pair of Lovers. 1480–1485","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGothaer_Liebespaar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Sri_Lankan_woman_and_child.jpg/500px-Sri_Lankan_woman_and_child.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Sri_Lankan_woman_and_child.jpg/500px-Sri_Lankan_woman_and_child.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grandmother and grandchild in Sri Lanka","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASri_Lankan_woman_and_child.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Le_mur_des_je_t%27aime%2C_Place_des_Abbesses%2C_Montmartre%2C_Paris_%28the_plaque_with_the_je_t%27aime_in_311_languages%29.jpg/500px-Le_mur_des_je_t%27aime%2C_Place_des_Abbesses%2C_Montmartre%2C_Paris_%28the_plaque_with_the_je_t%27aime_in_311_languages%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Le_mur_des_je_t%27aime%2C_Place_des_Abbesses%2C_Montmartre%2C_Paris_%28the_plaque_with_the_je_t%27aime_in_311_languages%29.jpg/1280px-Le_mur_des_je_t%27aime%2C_Place_des_Abbesses%2C_Montmartre%2C_Paris_%28the_plaque_with_the_je_t%27aime_in_311_languages